  "query_preview": "..(Tuesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 313 PM EDT SUN OCT 25 2026\n\nAt the start of the long term period, a 500 mb ridge will be \ncentered over the eastern CONUS, with troughing over the West. \nInitially, a shortwave will be lifting northeast along the \nperiphery of this ridge, with another shortwave following closely \nbehind. By Tuesday evening, these waves will merge, and this \nenergy will work to flatten the ridge. Flow will become \nsouthwesterly aloft, and though weak ridging looks to attempt to \nbuild in again by the end of the workweek, this pattern change \nwill be relatively short lived as a more well defined wave \napproaches the forecast area by the weekend.\n\nOverall, the weather looks to be very uneventful through the end \nof the workweek. At the surface, an area of low pressure will \ninitially be located in northwest Iowa, although this low will \nquickly lift north in accordance with aforementioned shortwave. A \nfrontal boundary previously stalled over Ohio will also lift north\nas a warm front at this time, with no effect on eastern \nKentucky's weather. Instead, surface high pressure to the \nsoutheast will dominate our local forecast, with light \nsouthwesterly winds at the surface. This pattern will allow for \nsome very modest moisture advection, with surface dewpoints \nincreasing into the upper 40s in some locations later in the \nworkweek. Meanwhile, a weak shortwave embedded within \nsouthwesterly flow aloft will move through the area late \nTuesday/early Wednesday, although in the absence of better \nmoisture, its main impact will be a brief increase in cloud cover.\nThis will keep lows warmer than in previous days, with valleys \nmore in the low 40s than the 30s, marking an end to any frost \nthreat for now, although nightly fog formation is still expected \nin typical valley locales. Otherwise, no impactful weather is \nexpected... only ample sunshine. Highs will be above average but \nalso pleasant, with temperatures rising into the low to mid 70s \neach day and lows in the 40s, with moderate ridge/valley \ntemperature splits developing overnight.\n\nA more notable pattern shift looks to arrive next weekend as a \nmore well defined shortwave pushes its way northeast toward the \nforecast area. A moist air mass will settle over eastern Kentucky,\nwith LREF mean PW values increasing to around 1\" by late \nSaturday. Around this time, PoPs begin to tick up. Nonetheless, \nthe vast majority of LREF members keep Halloween evening dry, with higher\nconfidence in rain on Sunday as LREF mean PW values peak in the \n1.2-1.3\" range. Even so, the exact timing of this system remains \nuncertain, and will require some refining in subsequent forecasts.",
